<Guide
  title="How to Pull the Tip Screen Prank on Your Waiter | TipFlip"
  headline="How to pull the tip screen prank (without being a jerk)"
  description="A step-by-step guide to flipping the tip screen on your server: when to do it, what to say, and how to make sure everyone is laughing at the end."
  published="2026-10-28"
>
  <p>
    You know the moment. The card reader spins around, and before you can blink you are choosing between 20%, 25% and
    30%. The tip screen prank flips that moment around: you hand <em>your</em> phone to the waiter, and it asks them to
    select a customer discount. Done well, it is one of the easiest laughs you can get at a restaurant. Done badly, it
    is just annoying. Here is how to do it well.
  </p>

  <h2>What is the tip screen prank?</h2>
  <p>
    The prank is a role reversal. A tip screen app like <a href="/">TipFlip</a> shows a screen that looks just like a
    point-of-sale tip prompt, with big blue buttons for 20%, 30% and 40%, a custom option, and a small gray
    "No discount" button. Your server picks a discount, signs for it with a finger, and gets a big green
    "Thank you!" check mark. Nobody actually gets money off. The joke is seeing the familiar screen pointed the other
    way.
  </p>

  <h2>Step by step</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Set it up before you go.</strong> Open TipFlip's settings and choose your discount amounts. You can also
      edit the welcome line and the "Are you sure?" message to fit your crowd.</li>
    <li><strong>Wait for the bill.</strong> The prank works best right when the check arrives, because that is when
      everyone is thinking about tip screens.</li>
    <li><strong>Hand it over with a straight face.</strong> Something like "Before we pay, would you mind filling this
      out?" works well. Let the screen do the rest.</li>
    <li><strong>Let them try to say no.</strong> If they tap "No discount", TipFlip asks "Are you sure?" and then makes
      them solve a math problem to prove they are not a heartless robot. This is usually where the laughing starts.</li>
    <li><strong>Collect the signature.</strong> If you turned signing on, they sign to "agree" to the discount, and the
      record is saved in your History with the date and time.</li>
    <li><strong>Tip fairly.</strong> This is the most important step. Pay the bill, leave a proper tip, and say thanks
      for being a good sport.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>When to do it, and when not to</h2>
  <p>Good moments:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>A relaxed dinner where you have already been chatting with your server.</li>
    <li>A coffee shop or bar you visit often, where the staff know you.</li>
    <li>A birthday or celebration, when the table is already in a joking mood.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Skip it when:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The restaurant is slammed and your server is running between tables.</li>
    <li>Something has already gone wrong with the meal. The joke can read as a complaint.</li>
    <li>You are not planning to tip. The prank only works when everyone knows it is a joke.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Make it funnier with your own messages</h2>
  <p>
    Every message in TipFlip can be edited. A few ideas:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Welcome line: "Please select a discount for your favorite customer".</li>
    <li>"Are you sure?" message: "Customers who get a discount are 99% more likely to tell their friends about you."</li>
    <li>Signature text: "By signing, I agree that this table is my favorite table tonight."</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Recording the moment</h2>
  <p>
    Reactions to the tip screen prank make great short videos. If you film, ask your server first and only post with
    their permission. Plenty of people are happy to be in on the joke, but nobody likes finding themselves online
    without being asked.
  </p>

  <h2>The golden rule</h2>
  <p>
    The tip screen prank pokes fun at tip screens, not at the people who carry them. Servers rely on tips, so the
    joke should always end with a fair one. If your server laughs, signs for 40% and still gets a generous tip, you did
    it right.
  </p>
